    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-73252",
      "knowledge_point_id": "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/1f2d8b9faac6c300b41902cea5065bf8bafb02c137232ee79779a4dc3ff67f33.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The function is continuous at $x=-1$\n\nII. The function is differentiable at $x=1$\n\nIII. The function is continuous at $x=2$",
      "worked_solution": "- Statement I is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$ So, it can't be differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III  are true.",
