      "reasoning": "All five answers match their own captured worked solutions and the earlier answers. q-73252: the graph is continuous at -1 and 2; its removable discontinuity at 1 prevents differentiability, so I and III hold. q-73250: the graph is discontinuous at 1, continuous at 0, and not differentiable at the corner at 0, so only II holds. q-101995: continuity at 0 does not guarantee differentiability; discontinuity at 1 prevents differentiability, and differentiability at -1 guarantees continuity, so II and III must hold. q-47681: the limit at 1 is 2 whereas f(1)=3, preventing continuity and differentiability there and differentiability throughout (-1,2), so only II holds. q-48737: f'(0)=0 on the horizontal segment; x=2 is a non-strict local maximum because nearby values are no larger, and the nondifferentiable join makes it a critical point. Thus all three statements hold. No submitted grade is provided; MA evidence comes from the captured worked solutions.",
