    "judgment": {
      "status": "confirmed",
      "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. q-123707: logarithmic growth is slower than n^{3/2}, so the sequence converges to 0. q-29744: [x^3/3] from -5 to 4 equals 63. q-113989: [-1/(3x^3)] from -3 to -1 equals 26/81. q-29673: [-2/(3x^{3/2})] from 1 to 2 equals (4-\\sqrt{2})/6. q-73252: the graph is continuous at -1 and 2, but its removable discontinuity at 1 prevents differentiability. q-73250: it is discontinuous at 1 and continuous at 0, where unequal one-sided slopes prevent differentiability. q-47669: h has a vertical tangent at 0; the other graphs show a corner, smooth curve, hole, or asymptote instead. q-101995: continuity alone does not imply differentiability, while discontinuity prevents differentiability and differentiability implies continuity. q-47681: the limit at 1 is 2 but f(1)=3, so only statement II holds. q-73410: the derivative vanishes precisely on (3,4); the endpoints are corners. q-73409: the derivative vanishes on (2,6) and fails to exist at the two joins, giving [2,6]. q-48737: the horizontal segment gives f'(0)=0; x=2 is a non-strict local maximum and a nondifferentiable join, so all statements hold. q-47919: B and D share the greatest height over the stated domain. q-99032: f'=3(x+1)(x+7), giving -1 and -7. q-131188: f'=6(x-4)(x+3), giving -3 and 4. q-131273: f'=8x(x-1)(x+1)e^{4x^2}, giving -1, 0, and 1. All three algebraic derivatives exist everywhere, so there are no additional critical points. No earlier submission or grade is supplied; confirmation is based on the captured worked solutions and independent mathematics.",
